{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TTJAHTvifhxy4WFSZBU2X1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"PreToolUse hook: HARD-DENY edits to the arbitration kernel.\n3\t\n4\tPLAN-019 P1-SEC-A (Phase 2 Wave 2A). Defense-in-depth on top of\n5\t`check_canonical_edit.py`. The canonical-edit sentinel is the first\n6\tline — it allows Owner-signed `approved.md` to unlock any canonical\n7\tgovernance path. But the arbiter of that sentinel (the hook source\n8\titself, plus the _lib modules it imports, plus the policies YAML it\n9\tconsumes) must NOT be sentinel-unlockable: an agent that can edit the\n10\tarbitration hook can remove itself from the guard list and then edit\n11\tanything.\n12\t\n13\t## What's in the kernel (HARD-DENY list)\n14\t\n15\t1. **The four governance hooks** — check_agent_spawn.py,\n16\t   check_canonical_edit.py, check_plan_edit.py, check_arbitration_kernel.py.\n17\t2. **Hook shared library primitives** — _lib/policy.py, _lib/redact.py,\n18\t   _lib/audit_emit.py, _lib/contract.py (neutral Event/Decision), the\n19\t   Claude adapter, and the PII pattern bank.\n20\t3. **Policy-as-code files** — .claude/policies/*.yaml + corresponding\n21\t   .jsonl fixtures (these drive policy_dispatch.py decisions).\n22\t4. **Code the v1.4.4 split moved, and the listed modules kernel code\n23\t   calls** — the seven helper packages that hold the code of seven\n24\t   facades since the v1.4.4 split (`_lib/canonical_edit/`,\n25\t   `_lib/_policy_parts/`, ...), each kernel in full, plus the five flat\n26\t   `_lib` modules and the live-adapter package listed in the\n27\t   KERNEL-DECOMPOSITION block of `_KERNEL_PATHS`.\n28\t\n29\tEach is matched via a dedicated glob in `_KERNEL_PATHS` below.\n30\t\n31\t## Escape hatch (Owner-only)\n32\t\n33\tIf the Owner must edit a kernel path (e.g. an ADR-approved refactor of\n34\tthe policy engine), they set BOTH of these env vars in the same shell\n35\tsession:\n36\t\n37\t    export CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE=\"<reason-slug>\"   # e.g. ADR-045-refactor\n38\t    export CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK=\"I-ACCEPT\"\n39\t\n40\tThe hook then allows the edit and emits a `veto_triggered` audit event\n41\twith `reason_code=kernel_override_used` so the override is traceable.\n42\tAbsent BOTH env vars, the edit is blocked regardless of any sentinel.\n43\t\n44\tPLAN-169 W3-K (ledger item E.2) made that sentence TRUE. It was not:\n45\t`main()` decided whether to audit the grant by reading a `decision` key\n46\tout of its own egress JSON, and `_emit_allow()` never writes one (a\n47\ttop-level \"allow\" is invalid on the PreToolUse wire — see its comment).\n48\t`decision == \"allow\"` was therefore False on every invocation and the\n49\tgrant audit was dead code from birth. Both the grant emits now key off\n50\t`_is_override_grant()`, the single source of truth for the grant fact.\n51\t\n52\tThis is stricter than sentinel gating on purpose: a spawned sub-agent\n53\tcannot set env vars in the parent Claude process, so the ACK token\n54\tcannot be forged by an agent spawn.\n55\t\n56\t## Fail-closed contract\n57\t\n58\tUNLIKE the sentinel hook, this hook is **fail-closed** on any\n59\tobservable internal error path (parse_error, unparseable payload):\n60\tif the hook cannot determine whether the path is a kernel file, it\n61\ttreats it as potentially-kernel and blocks with a synthetic reason.\n62\tThe only exception is a catastrophic Python exception (e.g. ImportError\n63\ton the adapter itself) — that still emits allow, because the sandbox\n64\tcannot function at all if the adapter is broken (you cannot recover\n65\tfrom a broken hook runtime via a blocking hook that depends on it).\n66\t\n67\tTests verify the fail-closed posture (block on missing file_path when\n68\tthe tool is Edit|Write|MultiEdit).\n69\t\"\"\"\n70\t\n71\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n72\t\n73\timport fnmatch\n74\timport hashlib\n75\timport json\n76\timport os\n77\timport re\n78\timport sys\n79\tfrom pathlib import Path\n80\tfrom typing import List, Optional\n81\t\n82\t_HOOKS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n83\tif str(_HOOKS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n84\t    sys.path.insert(0, str(_HOOKS_DIR))\n85\t\n86\t\n87\t# Arbitration kernel — full governance-enforcement surface.\n88\t# Each pattern uses the same segment-glob rules as _CANONICAL_GUARDS in\n89\t# check_canonical_edit.py: `*` matches a single segment, `**` matches\n90\t# zero or more segments. See _fnmatch_segments below.\n91\t_KERNEL_PATHS: List[str] = [\n92\t    # The four governance hooks (edit-gating surface).\n93\t    \".claude/hooks/check_agent_spawn.py\",\n94\t    \".claude/hooks/check_canonical_edit.py\",\n95\t    \".claude/hooks/check_plan_edit.py\",\n96\t    \".claude/hooks/check_arbitration_kernel.py\",\n97\t    # Skill-patch sentinel — same tier of power as the canonical guard.\n98\t    \".claude/hooks/check_skill_patch_sentinel.py\",\n99\t    # Hook library primitives that back the above hooks.\n100\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/contract.py\",\n101\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/policy.py\",\n102\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/policy_preprocessors.py\",\n103\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/redact.py\",\n104\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/pii_patterns.py\",\n105\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/audit_emit.py\",\n106\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/adapters/claude.py\",\n107\t    # The policy-as-code declarative surface — drives policy_dispatch.py.\n108\t    \".claude/policies/*.yaml\",\n109\t    \".claude/policies/*.yml\",\n110\t    \".claude/policies/fixtures/*.jsonl\",\n111\t    # The policy_dispatch dispatcher itself.\n112\t    \".claude/hooks/policy_dispatch.py\",\n113\t    # PLAN-045 Wave 1 P0-03 — native-subagent frontmatter under kernel.\n114\t    # ``.claude/agents/*.md`` carries the ``model:`` field read by\n115\t    # Claude Code's native subagent dispatcher. F-01-03 documented the\n116\t    # demotion-via-frontmatter attack: an agent that edits\n117\t    # ``security-engineer.md`` can silently route the VETO role to\n118\t    # Haiku. Adding this glob to the kernel HARD-DENY list means\n119\t    # frontmatter mutations require ``CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE + ACK``\n120\t    # (which a sub-agent cannot forge).\n121\t    \".claude/agents/*.md\",\n122\t    # PLAN-085 Wave E.2 — ADR-116 13-entry kernel-extension (S111).\n123\t    # Closes F-C2-008 single-edit catastrophic chain. Each entry maps to\n124\t    # >=1 of the 4 attack vectors per ADR-116 §2. PLAN-089 will revisit\n125\t    # the 22 deferred R-026 paths via a separate ADR.\n126\t    #\n127\t    # Attack-vector coverage per entry:\n128\t    # (1) settings.json     -> hook configuration disable (vector 1 + 2)\n129\t    # (2) _python-hook.sh   -> hook runtime swap (vector 1)\n130\t    # (3) gpg_verify.py     -> sentinel signature bypass (vector 2)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"178172ab-278a-4318-ba3b-1534bf22937a","uuid":"d615bea5-bdde-4479-a700-ba8139b98f25","timestamp":"2026-11-16T19:20:26.288Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/hooks/check_arbitration_kernel.py","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"PreToolUse hook: HARD-DENY edits to the arbitration kernel.\n\nPLAN-019 P1-SEC-A (Phase 2 Wave 2A). Defense-in-depth on top of\n`check_canonical_edit.py`. The canonical-edit sentinel is the first\nline — it allows Owner-signed `approved.md` to unlock any canonical\ngovernance path. But the arbiter of that sentinel (the hook source\nitself, plus the _lib modules it imports, plus the policies YAML it\nconsumes) must NOT be sentinel-unlockable: an agent that can edit the\narbitration hook can remove itself from the guard list and then edit\nanything.\n\n## What's in the kernel (HARD-DENY list)\n\n1. **The four governance hooks** — check_agent_spawn.py,\n   check_canonical_edit.py, check_plan_edit.py, check_arbitration_kernel.py.\n2. **Hook shared library primitives** — _lib/policy.py, _lib/redact.py,\n   _lib/audit_emit.py, _lib/contract.py (neutral Event/Decision), the\n   Claude adapter, and the PII pattern bank.\n3. **Policy-as-code files** — .claude/policies/*.yaml + corresponding\n   .jsonl fixtures (these drive policy_dispatch.py decisions).\n4. **Code the v1.4.4 split moved, and the listed modules kernel code\n   calls** — the seven helper packages that hold the code of seven\n   facades since the v1.4.4 split (`_lib/canonical_edit/`,\n   `_lib/_policy_parts/`, ...), each kernel in full, plus the five flat\n   `_lib` modules and the live-adapter package listed in the\n   KERNEL-DECOMPOSITION block of `_KERNEL_PATHS`.\n\nEach is matched via a dedicated glob in `_KERNEL_PATHS` below.\n\n## Escape hatch (Owner-only)\n\nIf the Owner must edit a kernel path (e.g. an ADR-approved refactor of\nthe policy engine), they set BOTH of these env vars in the same shell\nsession:\n\n    export CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE=\"<reason-slug>\"   # e.g. ADR-045-refactor\n    export CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK=\"I-ACCEPT\"\n\nThe hook then allows the edit and emits a `veto_triggered` audit event\nwith `reason_code=kernel_override_used` so the override is traceable.\nAbsent BOTH env vars, the edit is blocked regardless of any sentinel.\n\nPLAN-169 W3-K (ledger item E.2) made that sentence TRUE. It was not:\n`main()` decided whether to audit the grant by reading a `decision` key\nout of its own egress JSON, and `_emit_allow()` never writes one (a\ntop-level \"allow\" is invalid on the PreToolUse wire — see its comment).\n`decision == \"allow\"` was therefore False on every invocation and the\ngrant audit was dead code from birth. Both the grant emits now key off\n`_is_override_grant()`, the single source of truth for the grant fact.\n\nThis is stricter than sentinel gating on purpose: a spawned sub-agent\ncannot set env vars in the parent Claude process, so the ACK token\ncannot be forged by an agent spawn.\n\n## Fail-closed contract\n\nUNLIKE the sentinel hook, this hook is **fail-closed** on any\nobservable internal error path (parse_error, unparseable payload):\nif the hook cannot determine whether the path is a kernel file, it\ntreats it as potentially-kernel and blocks with a synthetic reason.\nThe only exception is a catastrophic Python exception (e.g. ImportError\non the adapter itself) — that still emits allow, because the sandbox\ncannot function at all if the adapter is broken (you cannot recover\nfrom a broken hook runtime via a blocking hook that depends on it).\n\nTests verify the fail-closed posture (block on missing file_path when\nthe tool is Edit|Write|MultiEdit).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport fnmatch\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport os\nimport re\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\n_HOOKS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\nif str(_HOOKS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n    sys.path.insert(0, str(_HOOKS_DIR))\n\n\n# Arbitration kernel — full governance-enforcement surface.\n# Each pattern uses the same segment-glob rules as _CANONICAL_GUARDS in\n# check_canonical_edit.py: `*` matches a single segment, `**` matches\n# zero or more segments. See _fnmatch_segments below.\n_KERNEL_PATHS: List[str] = [\n    # The four governance hooks (edit-gating surface).\n    \".claude/hooks/check_agent_spawn.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/check_canonical_edit.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/check_plan_edit.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/check_arbitration_kernel.py\",\n    # Skill-patch sentinel — same tier of power as the canonical guard.\n    \".claude/hooks/check_skill_patch_sentinel.py\",\n    # Hook library primitives that back the above hooks.\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/contract.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/policy.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/policy_preprocessors.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/redact.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/pii_patterns.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/audit_emit.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/adapters/claude.py\",\n    # The policy-as-code declarative surface — drives policy_dispatch.py.\n    \".claude/policies/*.yaml\",\n    \".claude/policies/*.yml\",\n    \".claude/policies/fixtures/*.jsonl\",\n    # The policy_dispatch dispatcher itself.\n    \".claude/hooks/policy_dispatch.py\",\n    # PLAN-045 Wave 1 P0-03 — native-subagent frontmatter under kernel.\n    # ``.claude/agents/*.md`` carries the ``model:`` field read by\n    # Claude Code's native subagent dispatcher. F-01-03 documented the\n    # demotion-via-frontmatter attack: an agent that edits\n    # ``security-engineer.md`` can silently route the VETO role to\n    # Haiku. Adding this glob to the kernel HARD-DENY list means\n    # frontmatter mutations require ``CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE + ACK``\n    # (which a sub-agent cannot forge).\n    \".claude/agents/*.md\",\n    # PLAN-085 Wave E.2 — ADR-116 13-entry kernel-extension (S111).\n    # Closes F-C2-008 single-edit catastrophic chain. Each entry maps to\n    # >=1 of the 4 attack vectors per ADR-116 §2. PLAN-089 will revisit\n    # the 22 deferred R-026 paths via a separate ADR.\n    #\n    # Attack-vector coverage per entry:\n    # (1) settings.json     -> hook configuration disable (vector 1 + 2)\n    # (2) _python-hook.sh   -> hook runtime swap (vector 1)\n    # (3) gpg_verify.py     -> sentinel signature bypass (vector 2)","numLines":130,"startLine":1,"totalLines":900}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TTJAHTvifhxy4WFSZBU2X1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch79aPP6Hq53ft7f5hJPk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01897nbJ42Vv22VrbDTkJpar","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/hooks/check_arbitration_kernel.py","offset":280,"limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":695,"cache_read_input_tokens":237642,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":695},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"178172ab-278a-4318-ba3b-1534bf22937a","uuid":"29d894bc-2547-4c60-b7b9-085dec589967","timestamp":"2026-11-16T19:20:27.749Z","request_id":"req_011Ch79aPBBbtBuumDEMsGz1","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01897nbJ42Vv22VrbDTkJpar":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/hooks/check_arbitration_kernel.py","offset":280,"limit":200}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01897nbJ42Vv22VrbDTkJpar","type":"tool_result","content":"280\t    # files the guards themselves TRUST, previously guarded by NEITHER\n281\t    # rail (verified on HEAD: canonical=False, kernel=False for both):\n282\t    #\n283\t    #   1. the ADR-121 sentinel signer REGISTRY — the identity root\n284\t    #      `check_canonical_edit._sentinel_grants_path` consults on the\n285\t    #      dual signer rail. Unguarded, an ordinary Edit silently disarms\n286\t    #      half the signer check, asymmetric with the legacy\n287\t    #      `.claude/sentinel-signers.txt` which HAS been kernel-guarded\n288\t    #      since ADR-116.\n289\t    #   2. the policy hash-pin drift MANIFEST — it pins the policy files\n290\t    #      that are themselves guarded; leaving the pinner rewritable\n291\t    #      makes the pin decorative (\"pin the pinner\").\n292\t    #\n293\t    # Both are KERNEL tier (no sentinel escape) precisely because a\n294\t    # sentinel that grants an edit to the signer registry could authorize\n295\t    # its own future signer. Deliberately NOT paired with an\n296\t    # \"absence ⇒ fail-closed\" inversion (C10): that would need a\n297\t    # definition of \"expected\" which is itself editable, and would make\n298\t    # DELETING a file the way to choose the posture.\n299\t    \".claude/security/sentinel-signers-registry.yaml\",\n300\t    \".claude/policies/.drift-manifest.json\",\n301\t    # KERNEL-DECOMPOSITION (v1.4.4 train, merge 888e9e6c). These rows\n302\t    # cover the code the v1.4.4 split moved out of seven kernel facades,\n303\t    # the plan-path predicate the train put in place of regexes in two\n304\t    # kernel files, and the modules listed below that kernel code calls.\n305\t    #   - Seven facades moved their code into helper packages. One recursive\n306\t    #     row per package makes that code HARD-DENY again, as it was inside\n307\t    #     the facades in v1.4.2, and a file added there later is HARD-DENY\n308\t    #     too (the federation-dir precedent above).\n309\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/canonical_edit/**/*\",  # check_canonical_edit.py\n310\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/agent_spawn/**/*\",  # check_agent_spawn.py\n311\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/_policy_parts/**/*\",  # _lib/policy.py\n312\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_emit_parts/**/*\",  # _lib/audit_emit.py\n313\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_log_parts/**/*\",  # audit_log.py\n314\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/**/*\",  # check_bash_safety.py\n315\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/_pair_rail_parts/**/*\",  # check_pair_rail.py\n316\t    #   - Five flat modules that kernel code calls:\n317\t    #     plan_paths.py — the plan-path predicate (is_plan_path,\n318\t    #       plan_id_from_path) that replaces the plan-path regexes the train\n319\t    #       removed from check_plan_edit.py and policy_preprocessors.py; it\n320\t    #       is HARD-DENY again, as those regexes were in v1.4.2;\n321\t    #     model_currency.py — is_veto_floor_model(), the VETO-floor predicate\n322\t    #       that kernel files now call as part of their VETO-floor check\n323\t    #       (the F-01-03 demotion check above rides on it);\n324\t    #     cli_currency.py, bash_indirect_guard.py, bash_indirect_telemetry.py\n325\t    #       — each added by this train and called by kernel code:\n326\t    #       cli_currency.py is the Codex payload-pin currency arm, which\n327\t    #       decides whether a payload SHA mismatch is accepted as\n328\t    #       \"compatible\" (called by the pair-rail code; ADR-182 trust root);\n329\t    #       bash_indirect_guard.py is the indirect-command BLOCK matcher and\n330\t    #       bash_indirect_telemetry.py the disabled-rail telemetry, both\n331\t    #       called by the bash-safety parts.\n332\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/model_currency.py\",\n333\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/cli_currency.py\",\n334\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/plan_paths.py\",\n335\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/bash_indirect_guard.py\",\n336\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/bash_indirect_telemetry.py\",\n337\t    #   - adapters/live/ — the live-adapter transport package, present since\n338\t    #     v1.0.0 and enrolled now because the kernel adapters/__init__.py\n339\t    #     loads it; its siblings above are already kernel.\n340\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/adapters/live/**/*\",\n341\t    # tests/test_kernel_decomposition_coverage.py checks that each package\n342\t    # below _lib/ that a kernel source reaches by an import statement or\n343\t    # by a / chain of string literals (resolved against the source's\n344\t    # directory or .claude/hooks) is kernel in full, and that each of the\n345\t    # five flat modules above is kernel and still imported by a kernel\n346\t    # source.\n347\t]\n348\t\n349\t\n350\tdef _emit_allow(system_message: Optional[str] = None) -> str:\n351\t    # Claude Code hook schema: top-level \"allow\" is NOT valid.\n352\t    # Emit empty {} or {\"systemMessage\": ...}.\n353\t    out: dict = {}\n354\t    if system_message:\n355\t        out[\"systemMessage\"] = system_message\n356\t    return json.dumps(out, ensure_ascii=False)\n357\t\n358\t\n359\tdef _emit_block(reason: str) -> str:\n360\t    return json.dumps(\n361\t        {\"decision\": \"block\", \"reason\": reason}, ensure_ascii=False\n362\t    )\n363\t\n364\t\n365\tdef _fnmatch_segments(path: str, pattern: str) -> bool:\n366\t    \"\"\"Segment-wise glob (copy of check_canonical_edit helper).\n367\t\n368\t    - `*` matches exactly one segment.\n369\t    - `**` matches zero or more segments.\n370\t    \"\"\"\n371\t    p_parts = path.split(\"/\")\n372\t    pat_parts = pattern.split(\"/\")\n373\t    return _match_segments(p_parts, pat_parts)\n374\t\n375\t\n376\tdef _match_segments(p_parts: List[str], pat_parts: List[str]) -> bool:\n377\t    if not pat_parts:\n378\t        return not p_parts\n379\t    head, rest = pat_parts[0], pat_parts[1:]\n380\t    if head == \"**\":\n381\t        for i in range(len(p_parts) + 1):\n382\t            if _match_segments(p_parts[i:], rest):\n383\t                return True\n384\t        return False\n385\t    if not p_parts:\n386\t        return False\n387\t    if head == \"*\" or fnmatch.fnmatchcase(p_parts[0], head):\n388\t        return _match_segments(p_parts[1:], rest)\n389\t    return False\n390\t\n391\t\n392\t# PLAN162_FIX_CASEFOLD (PLAN-162 consensus S1, P0 — BOTH rails).\n393\t#\n394\t# ``_match_segments`` matches with ``fnmatch.fnmatchcase``, i.e. exact\n395\t# case. On a case-INSENSITIVE filesystem — APFS, the default on this\n396\t# repo's platform — ``.claude/settings.JSON`` IS ``.claude/settings.json``:\n397\t# a write through the case variant OVERWRITES the guarded file, yet the\n398\t# variant classified non-kernel and sailed past the HARD-DENY the whole\n399\t# threat model assumes unreachable. Measured on HEAD:\n400\t#\n401\t#     .claude/settings.JSON             canonical=False  kernel=False\n402\t#     .claude/hooks/_lib/audit_emit.PY  canonical=False  kernel=False\n403\t#     .claude/sentinel-signers.TXT      canonical=False  kernel=False\n404\t#\n405\t# The fix normalizes BOTH the candidate rel and the guard patterns to\n406\t# lower case before matching. Over-classifying is the SAFE direction\n407\t# here (a false kernel-classification costs an override ceremony; a\n408\t# false non-classification is a silent bypass of the strictest rail).\n409\t#\n410\t# ``str.lower`` — not ``str.casefold`` — deliberately: casefold expands\n411\t# 'ß'→'ss', changing segment LENGTH and therefore what a glob matches.\n412\t# The exploited class is ASCII case variance, which ``lower()`` covers\n413\t# exactly, and identical inputs always fold identically, so the\n414\t# normalization can only ever ADD matches, never remove one.\n415\t#\n416\t# Precomputed at import so the hot path pays one ``str.lower()`` per\n417\t# classification rather than one per (segment x pattern).\n418\t_KERNEL_PATHS_FOLDED: List[str] = [pat.lower() for pat in _KERNEL_PATHS]\n419\t\n420\t\n421\tdef _is_kernel_path(path_str: str, repo_root: Path) -> bool:\n422\t    \"\"\"True if path_str resolves inside the kernel HARD-DENY list.\"\"\"\n423\t    if not path_str:\n424\t        return False\n425\t    p = Path(path_str)\n426\t    try:\n427\t        rel = str(p.resolve().relative_to(repo_root.resolve())).replace(\n428\t            os.sep, \"/\"\n429\t        )\n430\t    except (ValueError, OSError):\n431\t        # Path outside repo root → cannot be a kernel path by definition.\n432\t        return False\n433\t    # PLAN162_FIX_CASEFOLD — see the module note above.\n434\t    rel_folded = rel.lower()\n435\t    for pattern in _KERNEL_PATHS_FOLDED:\n436\t        if _fnmatch_segments(rel_folded, pattern):\n437\t            return True\n438\t    return False\n439\t\n440\t\n441\t_ACK_TOKEN = \"I-ACCEPT\"\n442\t_REASON_RE = re.compile(r\"^[A-Za-z0-9._\\-]{1,120}$\")\n443\t\n444\t\n445\tdef _override_granted(env: dict) -> bool:\n446\t    \"\"\"True only when BOTH env vars are set correctly.\n447\t\n448\t    `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE` must be a non-empty reason slug matching\n449\t    `[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,120}`. `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK` must equal the\n450\t    literal ACK token `I-ACCEPT`. Both must be present — the ACK alone\n451\t    (or a reason alone) does not grant override. This prevents\n452\t    accidental pastes from elsewhere from enabling the bypass.\n453\t    \"\"\"\n454\t    reason = (env.get(\"CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE\") or \"\").strip()\n455\t    ack = (env.get(\"CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK\") or \"\").strip()\n456\t    if not reason or not ack:\n457\t        return False\n458\t    if ack != _ACK_TOKEN:\n459\t        return False\n460\t    if not _REASON_RE.match(reason):\n461\t        return False\n462\t    return True\n463\t\n464\t\n465\t# --- PLAN-169 W3-K (ledger E.2) — honest grant-path audit ----------------\n466\t#\n467\t# The pre-W3-K callsite passed the free-text override REASON as ``plan_id``\n468\t# and a repo-relative PATH (silently truncated at 64 chars) as\n469\t# ``ceremony_sha`` — a field SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md:415 names a\n470\t# ceremony sha. Neither value was ever rejected: ``kernel_extension_landed``\n471\t# rides ``_EMIT_GENERIC_PASSTHROUGH`` (audit_emit.py:1751), so the kwargs\n472\t# survive the deny-by-default scrub verbatim. The event LANDED — with a\n473\t# path in a digest field and a reason slug in a plan field.\n474\t\n475\t_EDIT_TOOLS = frozenset({\"Edit\", \"Write\", \"MultiEdit\"})\n476\t_PLAN_ID_RE = re.compile(r\"^PLAN-[0-9]{3,}$\")\n477\t_PLAN_ID_UNKNOWN = \"unknown\"\n478\t\n479\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"178172ab-278a-4318-ba3b-1534bf22937a","uuid":"6c68c499-c177-4e1c-b790-e23142cbe17e","timestamp":"2026-11-16T19:20:27.759Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.n5R3O9/tree/.claude/hooks/check_arbitration_kernel.py","content":"    # files the guards themselves TRUST, previously guarded by NEITHER\n    # rail (verified on HEAD: canonical=False, kernel=False for both):\n    #\n    #   1. the ADR-121 sentinel signer REGISTRY — the identity root\n    #      `check_canonical_edit._sentinel_grants_path` consults on the\n    #      dual signer rail. Unguarded, an ordinary Edit silently disarms\n    #      half the signer check, asymmetric with the legacy\n    #      `.claude/sentinel-signers.txt` which HAS been kernel-guarded\n    #      since ADR-116.\n    #   2. the policy hash-pin drift MANIFEST — it pins the policy files\n    #      that are themselves guarded; leaving the pinner rewritable\n    #      makes the pin decorative (\"pin the pinner\").\n    #\n    # Both are KERNEL tier (no sentinel escape) precisely because a\n    # sentinel that grants an edit to the signer registry could authorize\n    # its own future signer. Deliberately NOT paired with an\n    # \"absence ⇒ fail-closed\" inversion (C10): that would need a\n    # definition of \"expected\" which is itself editable, and would make\n    # DELETING a file the way to choose the posture.\n    \".claude/security/sentinel-signers-registry.yaml\",\n    \".claude/policies/.drift-manifest.json\",\n    # KERNEL-DECOMPOSITION (v1.4.4 train, merge 888e9e6c). These rows\n    # cover the code the v1.4.4 split moved out of seven kernel facades,\n    # the plan-path predicate the train put in place of regexes in two\n    # kernel files, and the modules listed below that kernel code calls.\n    #   - Seven facades moved their code into helper packages. One recursive\n    #     row per package makes that code HARD-DENY again, as it was inside\n    #     the facades in v1.4.2, and a file added there later is HARD-DENY\n    #     too (the federation-dir precedent above).\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/canonical_edit/**/*\",  # check_canonical_edit.py\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/agent_spawn/**/*\",  # check_agent_spawn.py\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/_policy_parts/**/*\",  # _lib/policy.py\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_emit_parts/**/*\",  # _lib/audit_emit.py\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/_audit_log_parts/**/*\",  # audit_log.py\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/**/*\",  # check_bash_safety.py\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/_pair_rail_parts/**/*\",  # check_pair_rail.py\n    #   - Five flat modules that kernel code calls:\n    #     plan_paths.py — the plan-path predicate (is_plan_path,\n    #       plan_id_from_path) that replaces the plan-path regexes the train\n    #       removed from check_plan_edit.py and policy_preprocessors.py; it\n    #       is HARD-DENY again, as those regexes were in v1.4.2;\n    #     model_currency.py — is_veto_floor_model(), the VETO-floor predicate\n    #       that kernel files now call as part of their VETO-floor check\n    #       (the F-01-03 demotion check above rides on it);\n    #     cli_currency.py, bash_indirect_guard.py, bash_indirect_telemetry.py\n    #       — each added by this train and called by kernel code:\n    #       cli_currency.py is the Codex payload-pin currency arm, which\n    #       decides whether a payload SHA mismatch is accepted as\n    #       \"compatible\" (called by the pair-rail code; ADR-182 trust root);\n    #       bash_indirect_guard.py is the indirect-command BLOCK matcher and\n    #       bash_indirect_telemetry.py the disabled-rail telemetry, both\n    #       called by the bash-safety parts.\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/model_currency.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/cli_currency.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/plan_paths.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/bash_indirect_guard.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/bash_indirect_telemetry.py\",\n    #   - adapters/live/ — the live-adapter transport package, present since\n    #     v1.0.0 and enrolled now because the kernel adapters/__init__.py\n    #     loads it; its siblings above are already kernel.\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/adapters/live/**/*\",\n    # tests/test_kernel_decomposition_coverage.py checks that each package\n    # below _lib/ that a kernel source reaches by an import statement or\n    # by a / chain of string literals (resolved against the source's\n    # directory or .claude/hooks) is kernel in full, and that each of the\n    # five flat modules above is kernel and still imported by a kernel\n    # source.\n]\n\n\ndef _emit_allow(system_message: Optional[str] = None) -> str:\n    # Claude Code hook schema: top-level \"allow\" is NOT valid.\n    # Emit empty {} or {\"systemMessage\": ...}.\n    out: dict = {}\n    if system_message:\n        out[\"systemMessage\"] = system_message\n    return json.dumps(out, ensure_ascii=False)\n\n\ndef _emit_block(reason: str) -> str:\n    return json.dumps(\n        {\"decision\": \"block\", \"reason\": reason}, ensure_ascii=False\n    )\n\n\ndef _fnmatch_segments(path: str, pattern: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Segment-wise glob (copy of check_canonical_edit helper).\n\n    - `*` matches exactly one segment.\n    - `**` matches zero or more segments.\n    \"\"\"\n    p_parts = path.split(\"/\")\n    pat_parts = pattern.split(\"/\")\n    return _match_segments(p_parts, pat_parts)\n\n\ndef _match_segments(p_parts: List[str], pat_parts: List[str]) -> bool:\n    if not pat_parts:\n        return not p_parts\n    head, rest = pat_parts[0], pat_parts[1:]\n    if head == \"**\":\n        for i in range(len(p_parts) + 1):\n            if _match_segments(p_parts[i:], rest):\n                return True\n        return False\n    if not p_parts:\n        return False\n    if head == \"*\" or fnmatch.fnmatchcase(p_parts[0], head):\n        return _match_segments(p_parts[1:], rest)\n    return False\n\n\n# PLAN162_FIX_CASEFOLD (PLAN-162 consensus S1, P0 — BOTH rails).\n#\n# ``_match_segments`` matches with ``fnmatch.fnmatchcase``, i.e. exact\n# case. On a case-INSENSITIVE filesystem — APFS, the default on this\n# repo's platform — ``.claude/settings.JSON`` IS ``.claude/settings.json``:\n# a write through the case variant OVERWRITES the guarded file, yet the\n# variant classified non-kernel and sailed past the HARD-DENY the whole\n# threat model assumes unreachable. Measured on HEAD:\n#\n#     .claude/settings.JSON             canonical=False  kernel=False\n#     .claude/hooks/_lib/audit_emit.PY  canonical=False  kernel=False\n#     .claude/sentinel-signers.TXT      canonical=False  kernel=False\n#\n# The fix normalizes BOTH the candidate rel and the guard patterns to\n# lower case before matching. Over-classifying is the SAFE direction\n# here (a false kernel-classification costs an override ceremony; a\n# false non-classification is a silent bypass of the strictest rail).\n#\n# ``str.lower`` — not ``str.casefold`` — deliberately: casefold expands\n# 'ß'→'ss', changing segment LENGTH and therefore what a glob matches.\n# The exploited class is ASCII case variance, which ``lower()`` covers\n# exactly, and identical inputs always fold identically, so the\n# normalization can only ever ADD matches, never remove one.\n#\n# Precomputed at import so the hot path pays one ``str.lower()`` per\n# classification rather than one per (segment x pattern).\n_KERNEL_PATHS_FOLDED: List[str] = [pat.lower() for pat in _KERNEL_PATHS]\n\n\ndef _is_kernel_path(path_str: str, repo_root: Path) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"True if path_str resolves inside the kernel HARD-DENY list.\"\"\"\n    if not path_str:\n        return False\n    p = Path(path_str)\n    try:\n        rel = str(p.resolve().relative_to(repo_root.resolve())).replace(\n            os.sep, \"/\"\n        )\n    except (ValueError, OSError):\n        # Path outside repo root → cannot be a kernel path by definition.\n        return False\n    # PLAN162_FIX_CASEFOLD — see the module note above.\n    rel_folded = rel.lower()\n    for pattern in _KERNEL_PATHS_FOLDED:\n        if _fnmatch_segments(rel_folded, pattern):\n            return True\n    return False\n\n\n_ACK_TOKEN = \"I-ACCEPT\"\n_REASON_RE = re.compile(r\"^[A-Za-z0-9._\\-]{1,120}$\")\n\n\ndef _override_granted(env: dict) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"True only when BOTH env vars are set correctly.\n\n    `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE` must be a non-empty reason slug matching\n    `[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,120}`. `CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK` must equal the\n    literal ACK token `I-ACCEPT`. Both must be present — the ACK alone\n    (or a reason alone) does not grant override. This prevents\n    accidental pastes from elsewhere from enabling the bypass.\n    \"\"\"\n    reason = (env.get(\"CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE\") or \"\").strip()\n    ack = (env.get(\"CEO_KERNEL_OVERRIDE_ACK\") or \"\").strip()\n    if not reason or not ack:\n        return False\n    if ack != _ACK_TOKEN:\n        return False\n    if not _REASON_RE.match(reason):\n        return False\n    return True\n\n\n# --- PLAN-169 W3-K (ledger E.2) — honest grant-path audit ----------------\n#\n# The pre-W3-K callsite passed the free-text override REASON as ``plan_id``\n# and a repo-relative PATH (silently truncated at 64 chars) as\n# ``ceremony_sha`` — a field SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md:415 names a\n# ceremony sha. Neither value was ever rejected: ``kernel_extension_landed``\n# rides ``_EMIT_GENERIC_PASSTHROUGH`` (audit_emit.py:1751), so the kwargs\n# survive the deny-by-default scrub verbatim. The event LANDED — with a\n# path in a digest field and a reason slug in a plan field.\n\n_EDIT_TOOLS = frozenset({\"Edit\", \"Write\", \"MultiEdit\"})\n_PLAN_ID_RE = re.compile(r\"^PLAN-[0-9]{3,}$\")\n_PLAN_ID_UNKNOWN = \"unknown\"\n\n","numLines":200,"startLine":280,"totalLines":900}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01897nbJ42Vv22VrbDTkJpar","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
